{
  "_comment": "Dutch terms on the boards and the English used for them, once, so every catalogue says the same thing. Dutch government terms get their established English equivalent; where the Dutch abbreviation is the name foreigners meet (Woo, DigiD, BSN, KVK), it stays and the first use on a board spells it out. Product names (Dossiq, Pipelinq, Portaliq, Nextcloud, OpenProject, Zuiddrecht) are never translated. Names of people, streets, places, dates, times and amounts stay Dutch. English follows the Conduction voice: sentence case, no em-dashes, you and we, short sentences. British spelling (organisation, colour, licence).",
  "style": {
    "case": "Sentence case in every heading, button, tab and menu item. 'Mijn zaken' is 'My cases', never 'My Cases'.",
    "you": "Formal Dutch 'u' and informal 'je' both become 'you'. Keep the register of the board: resident-facing text is plain and warm, back-office text is terse.",
    "dates": "Dates, times and amounts are left as drawn (4 oktober 2026, 13:59, € 24,50). Weekday and month names in a running sentence stay Dutch too.",
    "names": "Person names, street names, place names, mail addresses and reference numbers stay as drawn.",
    "dashes": "No em-dash (—) and no double hyphen. Use a period, a comma or a colon.",
    "already_english": "A string that is already English is copied as it is, with sentence case and dashes fixed only when wrong."
  },
